Ladybird exposes storage.local to contexts with the storage permission,
through the shared completion helper. get() takes a key, an array
of keys, an object of defaults, or nothing, and results are built with
explicit property definitions, so __proto__ is an ordinary key. Changes
fire both storage.onChanged and storage.local.onChanged.

Chromium validates the same argument forms in StorageArea::
HandleFunctionCall(), and routes them through APIRequestHandler;
LocalStorageArea exposes QUOTA_BYTES and the area’s onChanged.
WebKit’s WebExtensionAPIStorageArea::get() extracts keys and
defaults the same way, and serializes values to JSON before
sending them. Gecko’s makeOnChangedEventTarget() gives the global event
two arguments and the area event one, as Ladybird does; but Gecko stores
structured clones, not JSON.
